<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhutan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kingdom of Bhutan, Druk Yul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhutan, officially the Kingdom of Bhutan, is a landlocked country in the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal</a> unified its valleys into a single state, defeating three Tibetan invasions. After the Duar War it ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dooars">Duars</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and the Wangchuck dynasty emerged as the monarchy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-punakha">Treaty of Punakha</a> of 1910 made Bhutan effectively a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, though it was never officially colonised. A 1949 treaty with India recognised each country's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and Bhutan joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhutan.jpg" alt="Bhutan" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bhutan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bhutan lies on the southern slopes of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, between the Tibet Autonomous Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the north and the Indian states of Sikkim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arunachal-pradesh">Arunachal Pradesh</a>. Its area is 38,394 square kilometres. The land consists mostly of steep mountains crossed by swift rivers that form deep valleys before draining into the Indian plains, and 98.8 percent of the country is covered by mountains. Elevation rises from 200 metres in the southern foothills to more than 7,000 metres, and the climate runs from subtropical in the south to year-round snow in the north.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a>, weapons and the remains of large stone structures show that Bhutan was inhabited as early as 2000 BCE, although no records survive from that time. Historians have theorised that a state called Lhomon, or Monyul, may have existed between 500 BCE and 600 CE. In the first millennium the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> spread to Bhutan from the Pala Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal</a> unified the valleys into a single state. He defeated three Tibetan invasions, subjugated rival religious schools, codified the Tsa Yig legal system and set up a government of theocratic and civil administrators. His successors acted as Bhutan's spiritual leaders.</p><p>Bhutan came under increasing British influence in the 19th century and ceded the Bengal Duars to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> after the Duar War. The Wangchuck dynasty emerged as the monarchy and pursued closer ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-punakha">Treaty of Punakha</a> of 1910 made Bhutan effectively a British protectorate, which guarded it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, but it was never directly colonised. After the Chinese Communist Revolution Bhutan signed a friendship treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at Darjeeling in 1949, under which each recognised the other's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Secular schooling for the general population, which had been monastic, was introduced in the 1960s. The first general elections for the National Assembly were held on 24 March 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a> is the capital and largest city, and the country has about 800,000 inhabitants. Bhutan is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> with a parliamentary government under the constitution of 2008, which provides for an elected National Assembly and a National Council. The national language is Dzongkha, written in a variant of the Tibetan script. Between two-thirds and three-quarters of the people follow Vajrayana Buddhism, the state religion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the second religion, most common in the south. The country is divided into twenty districts called Dzongkhag. Its main export is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydroelectricity">hydroelectricity</a>, supplied to states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhutan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade with Tibet, <b>Bhutan</b>, the hill peoples and Bengal brought salt, horses and cloth, though the kings kept it under tight control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bhutan</b> lifted its ban on television and the internet only in 1999, making it one of the last countries to introduce television; the king called it a step to modernisation but warned that its 'misuse' could erode traditional values.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Some Buddhists in <b>Bhutan</b> and Amdo practise both, and the Dongba rites of the Nakhi and the Hangui religion of the Pumi are believed to come from Bon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bon">Bon · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It has served as the religion of kingdoms and nations, from medieval Sri Lanka to modern Thailand and <b>Bhutan</b>, and has been invoked in movements of national identity, sometimes in ways that have deepened conflict with religious minorities.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In executive or semi-constitutional monarchies, such as Jordan, Morocco, Kuwait, Bahrain, <b>Bhutan</b>, Monaco and Liechtenstein, the monarch keeps substantial powers to appoint and dismiss ministers, veto laws or dissolve the assembly, and the cabinet may answer to both crown and parliament.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
